##############################################################################
## strings.json.tmpl -- the client-side translation dictionary.
##
## Generates jsondata/strings.json: a flat {"English phrase": "translated
## phrase"} map, resolved through the exact same $gettext(...) +
## skins/DivumWX/lang/<code>.conf mechanism as archive.json.tmpl's
## trend_desc field (see that file's own comment for the full explanation
## of how lang/<code>.conf selection works). The front-end
## (cardsBundleNew.js) fetches this once per page load and looks phrases
## up by their English text; a phrase with no entry for the active
## language falls back to itself, i.e. to English -- same one-string-at-a-
## time fallback behaviour the [Texts] mechanism already has, so a
## partial translation never breaks the page, it just leaves the
## untranslated phrases in English.
##
## Every value below is wrapped in json.dumps(...) rather than bare
## interpolation -- translators may paste text from Google Translate or
## similar that happens to contain a double quote, backslash, or other
## character JSON needs escaped; json.dumps handles that safely so a
## translation can't accidentally corrupt the JSON output.
##
## NOTE: $gettext(...) must always be called directly (as below), never
## assigned to a bare variable first (e.g. "#set $t = $gettext") --
## Cheetah auto-calls bare $name references, so that assignment would
## silently invoke gettext() with no arguments instead of storing the
## function. Confirmed against the real Cheetah engine while building
## this file.
##
## KEEPING THIS FILE IN SYNC:
## Every key below must exist as a matching quoted key under [Texts] in
## skins/DivumWX/lang/en.conf (the reference dictionary -- see its own
## header comment), and *should* exist in each of the other lang/<code>.conf
## files too, though a missing one just falls back to English rather than
## erroring. When a card gets converted to use $gettext(...) lookups
## instead of a hardcoded string, add its phrase here AND to en.conf in
## the same change -- the two are a paired dependency (see the DivumWX
## beta-fix handover's "paired-file dependencies failing silently" lesson)
## and drifting them apart is a silent bug, not a loud one.
##
## Phrases are grouped by the card/page they belong to purely as a reading
## aid for whoever's translating -- the JSON output itself is a flat
## dictionary; the grouping comments don't survive into strings.json.
##############################################################################
